      "problem": "$3$ is an integer number, and $\\sqrt{3}$ is **not** a rational number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $P:3\\text{ is an integer number}$\n\n- $Q:\\sqrt{3}\\text{ is a rational number}$",
      "worked_solution": "The negation is represented by the symbol \"$¬$\".\n\nThe sentence\n\n$3$ is an integer number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $P.$ \n\nThe sentence\n\n$\\sqrt{3}$ is **not** a rational number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n$3$ is an integer number, and $\\sqrt{3}$ is **not** a rational number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $P∧¬Q.$",
